@* What a row or card shows when an item has no cover art yet.

   A glyph on a flat grey block reads as a failed image load rather than "nothing linked here yet".
   Media libraries (Plex, Jellyfin, Spotify) use a text fallback derived from the title instead.
   It never looks broken, gives each placeholder a different face so a column stays scannable, and carries a little real information.

   A title starting with something other than a letter or digit (punctuation, an emoji) renders nothing rather than that character at 2.4rem, an empty tile being better than a loud meaningless one.
   Same for an item with no title yet. *@

@if (Initial is not null)
{
    <span class="kt-cover-initial" aria-hidden="true">@Initial</span>
}

@code {
    [Parameter] public string? Title { get; set; }

    private string? Initial
    {
        get
        {
            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Title)) return null;

            // Rune, not char: a surrogate pair (an emoji, a rarer CJK glyph) is two chars, and Title[0] would be half a code point, rendered as U+FFFD.
            foreach (var rune in Title.Trim().EnumerateRunes())
            {
                if (System.Text.Rune.IsLetterOrDigit(rune))
                {
                    return System.Text.Rune.ToUpperInvariant(rune).ToString();
                }

                // Only the first character decides: "…And Justice for All" stays blank rather than showing the A of its second word, which would misrepresent how it sorts and reads.
                return null;
            }

            return null;
        }
    }
}
